{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe three surface stories (CLI / MCP / library) each need the **same** decision: given a\nticket's current state and the acting session, should we warn that a *different* active\nsession holds this ticket? Duplicating that logic three ways would drift. This story\nprovides one **pure** detector plus the config toggle that gates it, so the surfaces only\nrender.\n\nOwnership is the *live* `claimed_session` (guaranteed live-only by the sibling reducer\nstory). The acting session id comes from the existing shared resolver\n`resolve_session_id()` (`src/rebar/_commands/session_id.py:71`), which returns the first\nnon-empty of `REBAR_SESSION_ID -> CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID -> OPENCODE_SESSION_ID ->\nSESSION_ID`, else `None`. Per the brainstorm decision, when the acting id is `None` we\nstay **silent** (we cannot prove we are a *different* session; the un-shimmed case is\ncommon — `docs/session-id-shims.md` gotcha G1), and a default-on config toggle lets an\noperator disable the warning if the resume/fork false positives prove noisy.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a new leaf module `src/rebar/_commands/cross_session.py` with a **pure** function:\n\n```\ndef cross_session_warning(\n    state: Mapping[str, Any], *, acting_session: str | None, enabled: bool\n) -> str | None\n```\n\nReturn `None` (silent) when any of: `enabled` is false; `acting_session` is falsy;\n`state.get(\"claimed_session\")` is falsy; or `acting_session == state[\"claimed_session\"]`.\nOtherwise return a single-line warning string naming the holder — the `claimed_session`\nid, and the `claim_harness` in parentheses when present — e.g.\n`ticket held by another session <sid> (harness <tag>)`. The function performs **no** I/O\n(no state load, no config read, no env read) so it is trivially unit-testable and reused\nverbatim by all three surfaces.\n\nAdd a thin convenience `cross_session_warning_for(ticket_id, *, tracker, repo_root)` that\ncomposes the pure function with the real reads: load compiled state (via the same read\npath `show`/`ready` use), `resolve_session_id()`, and the config toggle. The surfaces call\nthis convenience; unit tests target the pure core.\n\nAdd the config key `warnings.cross_session` (bool, **default `True`**) to the typed core\nschema following the existing `_documented(...)` dataclass-field pattern\n(`src/rebar/_config_schema.py:50`). `Config.from_mapping`\n(`src/rebar/_config_schema.py:703`) instantiates each section via\n`_SECTION_CLASSES[sect](**vals)` and then `cls(**{sect: ...})`, so a NEW `[warnings]`\nsection requires FOUR coordinated additions, not one: (1) a `WarningsConfig` dataclass with\n`cross_session: bool = _documented(True, ...)`; (2) a\n`warnings: WarningsConfig = field(default_factory=WarningsConfig)` field on the `Config`\ndataclass (`src/rebar/_config_schema.py:680-693`) — omitting it makes `cls(warnings=...)`\nraise `TypeError`; (3) a `\"warnings\": WarningsConfig` entry in the `_SECTION_CLASSES`\nregistry (`src/rebar/_config_schema.py:707`); and (4) the `warnings` section registered in\n`rebar._config_sections._SECTIONS` (the coercer registry consumed by `_config_schema.py:32`).\nRead it through the composed `Config` (the same load path every\nother typed key uses), so it honours `rebar.toml` + env + CLI overrides with no new read\nmechanism. The feature is fully **in-process** — no CI provider, no network — satisfying\n`project.portability`.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_commands/cross_session.py` — **new** module: the pure `cross_session_warning`\n  + the `cross_session_warning_for` convenience.\n- `src/rebar/_config_schema.py` — add the `[warnings]` section in THREE coordinated sites\n  `Config.from_mapping` requires: (1) a `WarningsConfig` dataclass with\n  `cross_session: bool = _documented(True, ...)`, (2) a\n  `warnings: WarningsConfig = field(default_factory=WarningsConfig)` field on the `Config`\n  dataclass (lines 680-693), and (3) a `\"warnings\": WarningsConfig` entry in\n  `_SECTION_CLASSES` (line 707).\n- `src/rebar/_config_sections.py` — register the `warnings` section (the `cross_session`\n  bool coercer) in `_SECTIONS`, the fourth wiring site the real load path needs.\n- `docs/config-reference.md` — document `warnings.cross_session` (default true) **and**\n  the deliberate silent-when-acting-id-unknown behavior as an evidence-revisitable choice.\n\n## Testing\n\nTier: **in-process unit test** — the pure detector is a validation-branch function\n(test-design §3), and the toggle is exercised through the **real config read path**\n(test-design §6 \"config-effect contrast\"), not a stubbed lookup.\n\n- Truth matrix over the pure `cross_session_warning`: warns (non-None, names holder)\n  exactly when `enabled and acting_session and claimed_session and acting_session !=\n  claimed_session`; silent (None) for each of the four suppression cases\n  (disabled / acting `None` / `claimed_session` absent / equal ids). Parameterized.\n- Message content: with `claim_harness` present the string includes both the id and the\n  harness tag; without it, just the id.\n- Config-effect contrast (real read path): construct a `Config` from a mapping with\n  `[warnings] cross_session=false` and one at the default (true); run\n  `cross_session_warning_for` against a fixture ticket held by session A while acting as\n  B, and assert the default warns and the `false` value is silent — proving the key is\n  wired through the actual `Config` load, not just parsed.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `cross_session_warning(state, acting_session=..., enabled=...)` returns a non-`None`\n  string containing the holder's `claimed_session` id exactly when\n  `enabled and acting_session and state[\"claimed_session\"] and acting_session !=\n  state[\"claimed_session\"]` (checked by a parameterized unit test matrix under\n  `make test`).\n- [ ] The detector returns `None` for every suppression case — `enabled=False`,\n  `acting_session=None`, `claimed_session` absent/falsy, or equal ids (checked by the same\n  parameterized test).\n- [ ] The returned string includes the `claim_harness` tag when present and omits it when\n  absent (checked by two assertions in a message-content test).\n- [ ] Config-effect: with `[warnings] cross_session=false` loaded through the real\n  `Config`, `cross_session_warning_for` is silent for an A-held ticket acted on by B; at\n  the default it warns (checked by `test_cross_session_config_toggle` exercising the real\n  config read path).\n- [ ] `docs/config-reference.md` documents `warnings.cross_session` (default true) and the\n  silent-when-acting-id-unknown revisitable decision (checked: `grep -n \"cross_session\"\n  docs/config-reference.md` matches, and the note contains \"revisit\")."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787877672821938001,"uuid":"2d034c05-818f-4826-8e80-b29697383b5a"}